the halo truss became world-renowned  when princess diana walked through a  minefield in angola  her son prince harry trodden her  footsteps on a visit to the country in  2019.  these projects are now under threat  the halo trust has been clearing minds  in northwest syria  left by syrian government forces and  islamic state  but the charity has lost its eu funding  since brexit  and has missed out on uk funding after  the government cut the aid budget  it will now stop work and pull out of  syria  our work really has something that the  british public can buy into  they can understand what we're doing you  can come and visit the minefield  see the impact that it has and blow up  the landmine  our work is incredibly good value for  money it seems to me that  someone needs to fund this work and  instead of bickering over budgets  we should be thinking about the people  who are actually suffering as a result  of this board  the uk aid budget was reduced last year  from 0.7  of gross national income to 0.5 percent  because of the financial cost of the  covered pandemic that's  small in percentage terms but massive in  impact on the ground  and not just in syria in a statement the  foreign office conceded that  the seismic impact of the kova 19  pandemic on the uk economy  has forced us to take tough but  necessary decisions  including temporarily reducing the  overall amount we spend on aid  that's meant that the uk has reduced the  amount it is sending to yemen  one of the world's worst humanitarian  crises  and british aid spending in libya the  sahel and afghanistan is also expected  to fall  these aid cuts are gonna cost lives it's  gonna  impact in some of the most fragile  places  in the world so places like syria  a 10-year war with 25 million people  not having access to basic services  the government says that the aid cut is  temporary  and that the budget will rise again when  the fiscal situation allows  there's no guarantee when that might be  though already  only a few months after the cut was  announced the consequences  are being felt around the world aleister  bunker sky news
 

